00:20:55.400 --> 00:20:56.700
It's in us from the beginning.

487
00:20:56.940 --> 00:20:57.160
Yeah.

488
00:20:57.880 --> 00:21:03.460
Well, let me tell you, a scientist named Leah Boker, who was a researcher at University

489
00:21:03.460 --> 00:21:08.320
of Cologne's Social Cognition Center, did an experiment taking...

490
00:21:08.320 --> 00:21:09.520
That's a tongue twister.

491
00:21:09.540 --> 00:21:10.500
It is a tongue twister.

492
00:21:10.800 --> 00:21:17.140
Leah took German soccer fans and had them watch videos of their biggest rival, who is

493
00:21:17.140 --> 00:21:21.360
the Dutch, the Dutch soccer team, missing penalty kicks.

494
00:21:21.580 --> 00:21:23.160
And she tracked their response.

495
00:21:23.660 --> 00:21:29.380
Then she showed them videos of the German team succeeding at penalty kicks.

496
00:21:29.380 --> 00:21:36.320
And what Leah found was that viewers were happier watching the Dutch team fail than they were watching

497
00:21:36.320 --> 00:21:37.700
their own team succeed.

498
00:21:38.640 --> 00:21:39.100
Whoa.

499
00:21:39.580 --> 00:21:39.900
Mm-hmm.

500
00:21:40.640 --> 00:21:40.820
Yeah.

501
00:21:40.960 --> 00:21:46.480
Watching your rival fail makes you feel better than your own success was the takeaway.

